  "prediction": "**Early Withdrawals from Retirement Savings: Incidence, Amount, and Factors**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) has released a report examining the incidence and amount of early withdrawals from retirement savings, including Individual Retirement Accounts (IRAs) and 401(k) plans. The report highlights the importance of understanding the factors that lead individuals to access their retirement savings early and identifies potential strategies to reduce this behavior.\n\n**Incidence and Amount of Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report estimates that in 2013, individuals ages 25 to 55 withdrew approximately $68.7 billion from their retirement accounts, with IRA owners accounting for 57% of these withdrawals. The report also found that 12% of IRA owners in this age group withdrew money early in 2013, with the amount withdrawn equivalent to 3% of their total IRA assets.\n\n**Factors Leading to Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report identifies several factors that contribute to early withdrawals from retirement savings, including:\n\n*   Age: Individuals ages 45 to 54 were more likely to withdraw from their IRAs than those in younger age groups.\n*   Education: Individuals with a high school education or less were more likely to withdraw from their retirement accounts.\n*   Family size: Individuals in larger family groups were more likely to withdraw from their retirement accounts.\n*   Marital status: Widowed, divorced, or separated individuals were more likely to withdraw from their retirement accounts.\n*   Economic characteristics: Individuals with lower household income, higher household debt, and lower personal cash reserves were more likely to withdraw from their retirement accounts.\n\n**Strategies to Reduce Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report identifies several strategies that may reduce early withdrawals from retirement savings, including:\n\n*   Automatic emergency savings accounts\n*   Automatic contributions to retirement accounts\n*   Financial education and counseling\n*   Simplified plan rules and procedures\n*   Increased transparency and communication with plan participants\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nThe report recommends that the Secretary of Labor direct the Assistant Secretary for the Employee Benefits Security Administration (EBSA) to revise the Form 5500 to require plan sponsors to report qualified plan loan offsets as a separate line item distinct from other types of distributions.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the importance of understanding the factors that lead individuals to access their retirement savings early and identifies potential strategies to reduce this behavior. By implementing these strategies, individuals and employers can work together to promote long-term retirement security.",
